The request is prepared (dry run — nothing was actually sent).

**Store both secrets** — `PATCH /accounts/demo/workers/scripts/demo-mailer/secrets-bulk` with body:
```json
{"secrets":{
  "MAIL_FROM":{"name":"MAIL_FROM","type":"secret_text","text":"receipts@mail.demo.example"},
  "REPLY_TO":{"name":"REPLY_TO","type":"secret_text","text":"support@demo.example"}
}}
```

The bulk endpoint uses JSON Merge Patch semantics: each key in the `secrets` map is the binding name, a secret object creates or updates it, `null` deletes it, and omitted names are left unchanged. So this sets `MAIL_FROM` and `REPLY_TO` and touches nothing else on the Worker. It's a single operation producing one new version with both changes included, which is why it's preferable to two separate `PUT /secrets` calls.

Two things worth flagging. This is a **deployment** — the new version is created and deployed immediately, so the Worker picks up both values on the next request. And these are `secret_text` bindings, meaning the values are write-only: they can't be read back through the API afterward, only overwritten or deleted, so keep the source of truth elsewhere.

I stored `MAIL_FROM` and `REPLY_TO` on the `demo-mailer` Worker in a single bulk secrets request, which creates one version containing both bindings. The call was a dry run, so no secrets were written and no version was deployed.

